!!Byblos!!
([[ /ˈbɪblɒs/ | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEnglish ]]
//BIB-loss//; Ancient Greek: Βύβλος), also known as !!Jbail!!, !!Jebeil!!,
!!Jbeil!! or !!Jubayl!! (Arabic: جُبَيْل,
[[ romanized | romanization-of-arabic ]]: //Jubayl//,
[[ locally | lebanese-arabic ]] //Jbeil//
[[ [ʒ(ə)beːl\] | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FLebanese_Arabic ]]),
is an ancient city in the [[ Keserwan-Jbeil Governorate ]] of [[ Lebanon ]].
The area is believed to have been first settled between 8800 and
7000 BC[[ ^1 ]] and continuously inhabited since 5000 BC.[[ ^2 ]] During its
history, Byblos was part of numerous cultures including
[[ Canaanite | canaan ]], [[ Egyptian | old-kingdom-of-egypt ]],
[[ Phoenician | phoenicia ]], [[ Assyrian | assyria ]],
[[ Persian | achaemenid-empire ]], [[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-period ]],
[[ Roman | roman-empire ]], [[ Umayyad | umayyad-caliphate ]],
[[ Abbasid | abbasid-caliphate ]], [[ Genoese | genoese-republic ]],
[[ Ayyubid | ayyubid-dynasty ]], [[ Mamluk | mamluk-sultanate ]] and
[[ Ottoman | ottoman-empire ]]. Urbanisation is thought to have begun during
the third millennium BC when it developed into a city,[[ ^3 ]][[ ^2 ]] making
it one of the
[[ oldest cities in the world | list-of-oldest-continuously-inhabited-cities ]],
if not the oldest. It is a [[ UNESCO ]] [[ World Heritage Site ]].[[ ^4 ]]

