    <td

The name appears as //kbnj// in [[ Egyptian | ancient-egyptian-language ]]
[[ hieroglyphic | egyptian-hieroglyphs ]] records going back to the
[[ 4th-dynasty | dynasty-iv ]] [[ pharaoh ]] [[ Sneferu ]]
(fl. 2600 BC)[[ ^7 ]] and as //Gubla// (𒁺𒆷) in the
[[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]] [[ cuneiform ]] [[ Amarna letters ]] to the
[[ 18th-dynasty | xviii-dynasty ]] pharaohs [[ Amenhotep III ]] and
[[ IV | amenhotep-iv ]]. In the 1st millennium BC, its name appeared in
[[ Phoenician | phoenician-language ]] and [[ Punic | punic-language ]]
inscriptions as //Gebal// (𐤂𐤁𐤋, gbl);[[ ^8 ]][[ ^9 ]] in the
[[ Hebrew | hebrew-language ]] [[ Bible ]] as //Geval// (גבל);[[ ^10 ]] and in
[[ Syriac | classical-syriac-language ]] as gbl (ܓܒܠ).
[[ Eusebius' //Onomasticon// | onomasticon-eusebius ]] stated that Byblos was
called "Gobel / Gebal" in Hebrew.[[ ^11 ]] The name seems to derive from gb
(𐤂𐤁, "[[ well | water-well ]]") and ʾl (𐤀𐤋, "[[ god | deity ]]"), the
latter a word that could variously refer to
[[ any of the Canaanite gods | canaanite-mythology ]] or to
[[ their leader in particular | el-deity ]]. The name thus seems to have meant
the "Well of the God" or "Source of the God".

Its present [[ Arabic | arabic-language ]] name //Jubayl// (جبيل) or
//J//(//e//)//beil// is a direct descendant of these earlier names, although
apparently modified by a misunderstanding of the name as the
[[ triliteral root ]] gbl or jbl, meaning "[[ mountain ]]". When the Arabic
form of the name is used, it is typically rendered //Jbeil//, //Jbail//, or
//Jbayl// in English. All of these, along with Byblos, are etymologically
related. During the [[ Crusades ]], this name appeared in Western records as
//Gibelet// or //Giblet//. This name was used for [[ Byblos Castle ]] and
[[ its associated lordship | lord-of-gibelet ]].

The Phoenician City, known to the Greeks as //Býblos// (Βύβλος) and to the
Romans as //Byblus//, was important for their import of [[ papyrus ]] out of
[[ Ancient Egypt ]][[ ^12 ]] – to the extent that "//byblos//" came to mean
"papyrus" in Greek. The [[ English | english-language ]] word
"//[[ Bible ]]//" ultimately derives from the Greek name of the city,
//Βύβλος// ('Βύblos / Byblos')//.//

== History and archaeology

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Jug_Byblos_Louvre_AO14707.jpg/250px-Jug_Byblos_Louvre_AO14707.jpg" height="225" width="190"

  <figcaption>Terracotta jug from Byblos (now in the Louvre), Late Bronze Age (1600–1200 BC)

Situated approximately 42 km (26 mi) north of [[ Beirut ]], Byblos holds a
strong allure for archaeologists due to its accumulations of various strata
resulting from countless centuries of human dwelling. The initial excavation
was conducted by [[ Ernest Renan ]] in 1860, documented in his work
//[[ "Mission de Phénicie" (1865–1874) | mission-de-phénicie ]]//. This was
succeeded by [[ Pierre Montet ]]'s efforts from 1921 to 1924, and later by
[[ Maurice Dunand ]], who continued excavations from 1925 for a span of forty
years.[[ ^13 ]][[ ^14 ]] Renan's expedition was to "provide the evidence that
the city did not move and that Gebeil is Byblos".[[ ^15 ]]

Fragments attributed to the semi-legendary pre-[[ Homeric ]]
[[ Phoenician | phoenicia ]] [[ priest | canaanite-religion ]]
[[ Sanchuniathon ]] say Byblos was the first city erected in [[ Phoenicia ]]
and was established by the god [[ Cronus ]].[[ ^16 ]] (Cronus was considered
the nearest equivalent to the Canaanite [[ Baal ]] / [[ Baal Hammon ]] in
[[ the syncretising system | interpretatio-graeca ]] used by the ancient
Greeks and Romans.) According to the writer [[ Philo of Byblos ]] (quoting
Sanchuniathon, and quoted in [[ Eusebius ]]), Byblos was founded by the
Phoenician shrine god [[ El | el-deity ]] (whom the [[ Greeks ]] identified
with their god [[ Cronus ]]). During the 3rd millennium BC, the first signs of
a town can be observed, with the remains of well-built houses of uniform size.
This was the period when the [[ Canaanite | canaan ]] civilization began to
develop.

=== Neolithic and Chalcolithic levels

[[ Neolithic ]] remains of some buildings can be observed at the site.
[[ Jacques Cauvin ]] published studies of [[ flint tools ]] from the
stratified Neolithic and [[ Chalcolithic ]] sites in 1962.[[ ^17 ]] Remains of
humans found in Chalcolithic burials have been published by
[[ Henri Victor Vallois ]] in 1937.[[ ^18 ]] Tombs from this era were
discussed by Emir [[ Maurice Chehab ]] in 1950.[[ ^19 ]] Early pottery found
at the tell was published by E.S. Boynton in 1960 with further studies by R.
Erich in 1954 and Van Liere and [[ Henri de Contenson ]] in
1964.[[ ^20 ]][[ ^21 ]][[ ^22 ]]

==== Dunand's five-level stratigraphy

Prehistoric settlements at Byblos were divided up by Dunand into the following
five periods, which were recently expanded and re-calibrated by
[[ Yosef Garfinkel ]] to correlate with [[ Tell es-Sultan ]] (Jericho):

- Early Neolithic (early phase) corresponding to the [[ Pre-Pottery Neolithic B ]] (PPNB) of Jericho, represented by plastered floors and [[ naviforme ]] technology, dated between 8800 and 7000 BC;
- Early Neolithic (late phase) corresponding to the [[ PNA | neolithic#neolithic-3--pottery-neolithic-pn ]] of [[ Tell es-Sultan ]] (Jericho) IX (also [[ Yarmukian ]]) between 6400 and 5800 BC, represented by [[ pottery ]], [[ sickle ]] blades, [[ figurines | figurine ]] and small points;
- Middle Neolithic corresponding to the [[ PNB | neolithic#neolithic-3--pottery-neolithic-pn ]] of [[ Tell es-Sultan ]] (Jericho) VIII and represented by pottery, dated between 5800 and 5300 BC;
- Late Neolithic corresponding to the Middle [[ Chalcolithic ]] of [[ Beth Shean ]] and represented by pottery, [[ stone vessels | stone-vessels-in-ancient-judaea ]], [[ silos | silo ]], [[ chamber tombs | chamber-tomb ]] and seals, dated between 5300 and 4500 BC;
- Early Chalcolithic corresponding to the Late Chalcolithic of [[ Ghassulian ]], represented by [[ jar burials | jar-burial ]], pierced flint, churn and a violin figurine, dated to between 4500 and 3600 BC and,
- Late Chalcolithic corresponding to the Early [[ Bronze Age ]], represented by [[ architecture ]] and [[ cylinder seal ]] impressions, dated to between 3600 and 3100 BC.[[ ^1 ]]

The site first appears to have been settled during the
[[ Pre-Pottery Neolithic B ]] period, approximately 8800 to 7000
BC[[ ^1 ]][[ ^23 ]] (Durand's Early Neolithic).

Early Neolithic Byblos was a later settlement than others in the
[[ Beqaa Valley ]] such as [[ Labweh ]] and [[ Ard Tlaili ]]. It was located
on the seaward slope of the larger of the two hills that used to compose
ancient Byblos, with a watered valley in between.[[ ^24 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Shir_Gefaess_der_DFBW.jpg/250px-Shir_Gefaess_der_DFBW.jpg" height="225" width="250"

  <figcaption>Dark faced burnished ware pottery from [[ Shir | shir-neolithic-site ]], in [[ Syria ]]

The original site spread down into the valley and covered an area of 1.2 ha
(3.0 acres) providing fertile soils and a protected landing place for boats.
Dunand discovered around twenty houses although some of the settlement was
suggested to have been lost to the sea, robbed or
destroyed.[[ ^14 ]][[ ^25 ]][[ ^26 ]][[ ^27 ]][[ ^28 ]][[ ^29 ]][[ ^30 ]]
Dwellings were rectangular with plastered floors, [[ pottery ]] was usually
[[ Dark faced burnished ware ]] with some shell impressions.[[ ^31 ]]

The Middle Neolithic was a smaller settlement of no more than 0.15 ha (0.37
acres) adjacent to the older site. The pottery was more developed with red
washes and more varied forms and elaborate decorations, buildings were poorer
with unplastered floors.

The Late Neolithic period showed development from the middle in building
design, a wider range of more developed flint tools and a far larger variety
of pottery with fabrication including silica. The Late Chalcolithic featured
developments of "[[ Canaanite blades | canaanean-blade ]]" and fan scrapers.
Adult burials in jars started to appear along with metal in the form of one
[[ copper ]] hook, found in a jar. Some jars were lined with white plaster
that was applied and self-hardened after firing.[[ ^32 ]] Copper appeared more
frequently in the Late Chalcolithic period along with multiple burials in
tombs and jar handles with impressed signs.[[ ^20 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Byblos_5.jpg/250px-Byblos_5.jpg" height="285" width="190"

  <figcaption>Byblos

=== Early Bronze

According to [[ Lorenzo Nigro ]], Byblos moved from being a fishermen's
village to its earlier urban form at the beginning of the third millennium
BC.[[ ^33 ]] Early Bronze Age remains were characterised by the development of
Byblos combed ware and a lithic assemblage studied by Jacques
Cauvin.[[ ^24 ]][[ ^34 ]]

Watson Mills and Roger Bullard suggest that during the
[[ Old Kingdom of Egypt ]] and [[ Middle Kingdom of Egypt ]] Byblos was
virtually an Egyptian colony.[[ ^13 ]] The growing city was a wealthy one and
seems to have been an ally (among "those who are on his waters") of
[[ Egypt ]] for many centuries. [[ First Dynasty | first-dynasty-of-egypt ]]
tombs used timbers from Byblos. One of the oldest Egyptian words for an
oceangoing boat was "Byblos ship". Archaeologists have recovered
[[ Egyptian | ancient-egypt ]]-made artifacts as old as a vessel fragment
bearing the name of the [[ Second dynasty | second-dynasty-of-egypt ]] ruler
[[ Khasekhemwy ]], although this "may easily have reached Byblos through trade
and/or at a later period".[[ ^35 ]]

- EB IA (3600-3300 BCE): Rural/fisherman's village (Dunand Installation IIb from Chalcolithic to EB IA) with a shrine erected at the natural spring.[[ ^36 ]] The spring was gradually built up as a sacred well.[[ ^37 ]]
- EB IB (3300-3000 BCE): Proto-urban town with a temple compound. (Dunand Installation III).[[ ^36 ]]

=== Middle Bronze

In the Middle Bronze IIA, objects have been found at Byblos naming the
[[ 12th Dynasty | twelfth-dynasty-of-egypt ]] king [[ Senusret II ]],[[ ^38 ]]
the [[ 13th Dynasty | thirteenth-dynasty-of-egypt ]] Egyptian king
[[ Neferhotep I ]].

=== Late Bronze

During the Late Bronze Age, the rulers of Byblos maintained close
relationships with the New Kingdom pharaohs of Ancient Egypt.

==== Amarna period

Around 1350 BC, the [[ Amarna letters ]] include 60 letters from
[[ Rib-Hadda ]] and his successor [[ Ili-Rapih ]] who were rulers of Byblos,
writing to the Egyptian government. This is mainly due to Rib-Hadda's constant
pleas for military assistance from [[ Akhenaten ]]. They also deal with the
conquest of neighbouring city-states by the [[ Habiru ]].

==== Ramesside period

It appears Egyptian contact peaked during the
[[ 19th dynasty | nineteenth-dynasty-of-egypt ]], only to decline during the
[[ 20th | twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt ]] and
[[ 21st | twenty-first-dynasty-of-egypt ]] dynasties. In addition, when the
[[ New Kingdom | new-kingdom-of-egypt ]] collapsed in the 11th century BC,
Byblos ceased being a colony and became the foremost city of
Phoenicia.[[ ^39 ]] Although the archaeological evidence seems to indicate a
brief resurgence during the [[ 22nd | twenty-second-dynasty-of-egypt ]] and
[[ 23rd | twenty-third-dynasty-of-egypt ]] dynasties, it is clear after the
[[ Third Intermediate Period | third-intermediate-period-of-egypt ]] the
Egyptians started favouring [[ Tyre | tyre-lebanon ]] and [[ Sidon ]] instead
of Byblos.[[ ^40 ]]

=== Iron Age

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Traditional_lebanese_house_at_Byblos.jpg/250px-Traditional_lebanese_house_at_Byblos.jpg" height="168" width="250"

  <figcaption>Traditional Lebanese house overlooking the Mediterranean sea, Byblos. This house is within the antiquities complex and illustrates the modern ground level concerning excavations

==== Phoenician period

Archaeological evidence at Byblos, particularly the five
[[ Byblian royal inscriptions ]] dating back to around 1150–950 BC, shows
existence of a [[ Phoenician alphabet ]] of twenty-two characters; an
important example is the [[ Ahiram sarcophagus ]]. The use of the alphabet was
spread by Phoenician merchants through their maritime trade into parts of
North Africa and Europe. One of the most important monuments of this period is
the [[ Temple of the Obelisks ]], dedicated to the
[[ Canaanite war god | ancient-canaanite-religion ]] [[ Resheph ]], but this
had fallen into ruins by the time of [[ Alexander the Great ]].

==== Assyrian period

In the [[ Assyrian | assyria ]] period, Sibittibaal of Byblos became tributary
to [[ Tiglath-Pileser III ]] in 738 BC, and in 701 BC, when [[ Sennacherib ]]
conquered all [[ Phoenicia ]], the king of Byblos was Urumilki. Byblos was
also subject to Assyrian kings [[ Esarhaddon ]] (r. 681–669 BC) and
[[ Ashurbanipal ]] (r. 668–627 BC), under its kings Milkiasaph and Yehawmelek.

==== Persian period

In the [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] (538–332 BC), Byblos was the fourth of four
Phoenician vassal kingdoms established by the Persians; the first three being
[[ Sidon ]], [[ Tyr | tyre-lebanon ]], and [[ Arwad ]].

=== Classical antiquity

[[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-period ]] rule came with the arrival of
[[ Alexander the Great ]] in the area in 332 BC. [[ Coinage | currency ]] was
in use, and there is abundant evidence of continued trade with other
Mediterranean countries.

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Roman_era_Phoenecia.png/250px-Roman_era_Phoenecia.png" height="136" width="250"

  <figcaption>Phoenicia in [[ late antiquity ]], from the [[ Peutinger map ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Byblos-109933.jpg/250px-Byblos-109933.jpg" height="188" width="250"

  <figcaption>Ruins at port.

During the [[ Greco-Roman | ancient-rome ]] period, the temple of
[[ Resheph ]] was elaborately rebuilt, and the city, though smaller than its
neighbours such as Tyrus and Zidonia, was a centre for the cult of
[[ Adonis ]].

King [[ Herod | herod-the-great ]] of [[ Judaea | herodian-kingdom ]], known
for his extensive building projects, including beyond his own kingdom,
constructed a city wall for Byblos.[[ ^41 ]]

In the 3rd century, a small but impressive theatre was constructed. With the
rise of [[ Christianity ]], a [[ bishopric | episcopal-see ]] was established
in Byblos, and the town grew rapidly. Although a
[[ Sasanian | sasanian-empire ]] colony is known to have been established in
the region following the [[ early Muslim conquests ]] of 636, there is little
archaeological evidence for it. Trade with [[ Europe ]] effectively dried up,
and it was not until the coming of the [[ First Crusade ]] in 1098 that
prosperity returned to Byblos, known then as Gibelet or Giblet.

=== Crusader, Mamluk, Ottoman period

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d8/Byblos_Castle.jpg/250px-Byblos_Castle.jpg" height="253" width="190"

  <figcaption>Crusader Fort

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/St_JohnByblos.jpg/250px-St_JohnByblos.jpg" height="188" width="250"

  <figcaption>The Crusades-era [[ Church of St. John-Mark | st.-johns-cathedral-byblos ]] in Byblos

In the 12th and 13th century, Byblos became part of the
[[ County of Tripoli ]], a [[ Crusader state | crusader-states ]] connected
to, but
[[ largely independent | vassals-of-the-kingdom-of-jerusalem#northern-states ]]
from, the [[ Crusader | crusade ]] [[ Kingdom of Jerusalem ]].

As Gibelet or Giblet, it came under the rule of the [[ Genoese | genoa ]]
[[ Embriaco family ]], who created for themselves the
[[ Lordship of Gibeletto ]], first as administrators of the city in the name
of the [[ Republic of Genoa ]], and then as a hereditary fief, undertaking to
pay an annual fee to Genoa and the
[[ church of San Lorenzo | genoa-cathedral ]] (Genoa's Cathedral).[[ ^42 ]]

The Embriaco family's residence, the [[ Byblos Castle ]], along with the
fortified town, served as an important military base for the Crusaders. The
remains of the castle are among the most impressive architectural structures
now visible in the town centre. The town was taken by [[ Saladin ]] in 1187,
re-taken by the Crusaders, and conquered by [[ Baibars ]] in 1266, but it
remained in the possession of the Embriacos until around 1300.

Having voluntarily surrendered to the Mamluks, the city was relatively spared
from looting following its capture.[[ ^43 ]] Its fortifications were
subsequently restored by [[ Baybars ]].[[ ^44 ]] From 1516 until 1918, the
town and the whole region became part of the [[ Ottoman Empire ]].

=== Contemporary history

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Old_streets.jpg/250px-Old_streets.jpg" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>Byblos Historic Quarter

Byblos and all of Lebanon were placed under
[[ French Mandate | french-mandate-of-lebanon ]] from 1920 until 1943 when
Lebanon achieved independence. The [[ 2006 Lebanon War ]] negatively affected
the ancient city by covering its harbour and town walls with an oil slick that
was the result of an oil spill from a nearby power plant.[[ ^45 ]]

During the [[ 2024 Israeli invasion of Lebanon ]], [[ UNESCO ]] gave Byblos
and 33 other cultural sites enhanced protection to safeguard them against
[[ damage | destruction-of-cultural-heritage-during-the-israeli-invasion-of-lebanon ]].[[ ^46 ]]
An exhibition of 400 artefacts,
//[[ Byblos, Millennia-old City of Lebanon ]]//, from Byblos was held at the
[[ Institut du monde arabe ]] in Paris in 2026.[[ ^47 ]]

== Demographics

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/ByblosSouk.jpg/250px-ByblosSouk.jpg" height="188" width="250"

  <figcaption>The old souk in Byblos, [[ Lebanon ]]

Byblos's inhabitants are significantly Christian, mostly
[[ Maronite | lebanese-maronite-christians ]], with minorities of
[[ Armenian Apostolic | armenian-apostolic-church ]],
[[ Greek Orthodox | greek-orthodox-church-of-antioch ]], and
[[ Greek Catholics | melkite-greek-catholic-church ]]. There is also a
minority of [[ Shia | lebanese-shia-muslims ]] and
[[ Sunni | lebanese-sunni-muslims ]] Muslims. It is said that the
predominantly Shi`i city of [[ Bint Jbeil ]] ("Daughter of Byblos") in
Southern Lebanon was founded by Shi`a migrants from Byblos. Byblos has three
representatives in the [[ Parliament of Lebanon ]]: two Maronites and one
Shi`i Muslim.[[ ^48 ]][[ ^49 ]]

== Education

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Sultan_Abdul_Majid_mosque_in_Byblos%2C_Lebanon_%28for_women_only%29.JPG/250px-Sultan_Abdul_Majid_mosque_in_Byblos%2C_Lebanon_%28for_women_only%29.JPG" height="188" width="250"

  <figcaption>Sultan Abdulmejid mosque in Byblos, Lebanon

Byblos is home to the professional schools of the
[[ Lebanese American University ]] (LAU). The LAU Byblos Campus houses the
Medical School, the Engineering School, the School of Architecture and Design,
the Pharmacy School, which offers the only Pharm.D. Program outside the United
States accredited by the
[[ Accreditation Council for Pharmacy Education (ACPE) | accreditation-council-for-pharmacy-education ]],[[ ^50 ]]
the School of Business, and the School of Arts and Sciences.

== Tourism

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Byblos_beach.jpg/250px-Byblos_beach.jpg" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>Byblos public beach

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/ByblosSite1.jpg/250px-ByblosSite1.jpg" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>The King's Spring

Byblos is re-emerging as an upscale touristic hub.[[ ^51 ]] With its ancient
[[ port | byblos-port ]], [[ Phoenician | phoenicia ]], Roman, and Crusader
ruins, sandy beaches and the picturesque mountains that surround it make it an
ideal tourist destination. The city is known for its fish restaurants,
open-air bars, and outdoor cafes. Yachts cruise into its harbor today as they
did in the 1960s and 1970s when [[ Marlon Brando ]] and [[ Frank Sinatra ]]
were regular visitors to the city.[[ ^51 ]] Byblos was crowned as the "Arab
Tour Capital" for the year 2016 by the Lebanese minister of tourism in the
[[ Grand Serail | grand-serail-of-beirut ]] in [[ Beirut ]]. Byblos was chosen
by [[ Condé Nast Traveler ]] as the second best city in the [[ Middle East ]]
for 2012, beating [[ Tel Aviv ]] and [[ Dubai ]],[[ ^52 ]] and by the
[[ World Tourism Organization ]] as the best Arab [[ tourist city ]] for
2013.[[ ^53 ]]

== The Byblos archaeological site

- //Ain el-Malik// or //King's Spring//, about 20 m deep, is a large cavity accessible by spiral stairs. Once it supplied the city with water.[[ ^54 ]] According to [[ Plutarch ]]'s version of the Egyptian [[ Osiris myth ]], the king's servants met [[ Isis ]] on the stairs of the spring and took her to the royal palace, where she found the body of her husband [[ Osiris ]] embedded in one of the palace pillars.[[ ^55 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/L_Shaped_Byblos.jpg/250px-L_Shaped_Byblos.jpg" height="153" width="250"

  <figcaption>The L-shaped Temple

- The //L-shaped Temple// was erected about 2700 BC.

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/ByblosObeliskTemple.jpg/250px-ByblosObeliskTemple.jpg" height="188" width="250"

  <figcaption>The Temple of the Obelisks

- The [[ Temple of the Obelisks ]], originally built in 1600–1200 BC on top of the “L-shaped temple,” was moved by archaeologists to its present location. The many small obelisks found in this temple were used as religious offerings. The sanctuary contained a large number of [[ human figurines made of bronze covered with gold leaf | byblos-figurines ]], which are now displayed in the [[ National Museum of Beirut ]].
- The [[ royal necropolis | royal-necropolis-of-byblos ]] dates back to the second millennium BC and contains tombs of the Byblos kings, including King [[ Ahiram ]].
- The [[ Bronze Age necropolis | bronze-age-necropolis-of-byblos ]] of the Gebalite elite, discovered in 2019 at the southern outskirts of the ancient city acropolis.[[ ^38 ]]
- The [[ Roman theater | roman-theatre-of-byblos ]] was built around AD 218.

== Other historic buildings

- !!Byblos Wax Museum!!

The Byblos Wax Museum displays wax statues of characters whose dates of origin
range from [[ Phoenician | phoenicia ]] times to current days.

- !!Byblos Fossil Museum!!

The Byblos Fossil Museum has a collection of fossilised fish, sharks, eel,
flying fish, and other marine life, some of which are millions of years old.

- !!Medieval city wall!!

The old medieval part of Byblos is surrounded by walls running about 270m from
east to west and 200m from north to south.

- !!Byblos Castle!!

Byblos Castle was built by the Crusaders in the 12th century. It is located in
the archaeological site near the port.

- !![[ St John the Baptist Church | st.-johns-cathedral-byblos ]]!!

Work on the church started during the Crusades in 1115. It was considered a
cathedral and was partially destroyed during an earthquake in AD 1170. It was
later given to the Maronite bishop as a gift by Prince
[[ Yusuf Shihab ]].[[ ^56 ]]

- !!Sultan Abduljid Mosque!!

The old mosque by the Castle dates back to the [[ Mamluk ]] period, and
adopted the name of Sultan [[ Abdulmejid I ]] after he renovated it.

- !!Historic Quarter and Souks!!

In the southeast section of the historic city, near the entrance of the
archaeological site, is an old market.

- !!Byblos International Festival!!

This summer music festival is an annual event that takes place in the historic
quarter.

- !!Temple of Baalat Gebal!!

- !!Aram Bezikian Museum!!

The Armenian Genocide Orphans' Aram Bezikian Museum is a museum dedicated to
preserving the memory of the Armenian Genocide and its survivors.[[ ^57 ]]

== Notable people

- [[ Majdi Allawi ]] (born 1970), Maronite priest, association founder
- [[ Jihad Azour ]] (born 1966), economist

== Twin towns – sister cities

Byblos is [[ twinned | sister-city ]] with:

- [[ Bonifacio | bonifacio-corse-du-sud ]], France
- [[ Mangalia ]], Romania
- [[ Orange | orange-vaucluse ]], France
- [[ Patras ]], Greece

